WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:06.800
Secret swords, initials carved in the background,&nbsp;
and celebrity cameos so small you didn't even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:12.356
notice. These are just a few of the Easter&nbsp;
Eggs you missed while watching Game of Thrones.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.600
In the books, the Iron Throne is a monstrosity,&nbsp;
sitting over 20 feet tall and made from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:23.760
blades of thousands of opponents vanquished by&nbsp;
Aegon Targaryen. Obviously, a throne that size&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.560
wouldn't be easy to film for television, so&nbsp;
the famous prop used in the Red Keep set is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:33.280
much smaller than the one described in the books.&nbsp;
The showrunners knew this might cause complaints&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:37.600
among fans of the novels, so they threw in&nbsp;
this fun little bit of meta-dialogue in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.560
Season 3 episode "The Climb" between Varys&nbsp;
and Littlefinger. Varys solemnly mentions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:46.960
the legend of the the thousand blades, and&nbsp;
Littlefinger has a pretty cheeky response:

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:54.358
"There aren't a thousand blades. There&nbsp;
aren't even two hundred. I've counted."

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:59.840
Speaking of the Iron Throne, did you know that&nbsp;
some of those vanquished foes apparently include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:04.560
Ian McKellen and Orlando Bloom? That's&nbsp;
right — eagle-eyed viewers have spotted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.560
a few "famous" swords hidden among&nbsp;
the generic blades in the Iron Throne.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:13.040
Notably, you can see Glamdring, wielded by&nbsp;
Gandalf in the Lord of the Rings movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:18.000
There are also swords from both Kingdom of&nbsp;
Heaven and Robin Hood: Prince of Thieves.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.040
Following Ned Stark's execution at the hands&nbsp;
of King Joffrey, the new monarch shows us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.920
more of his sadistic streak when he forces Sansa&nbsp;
Stark to come and look at her father's head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.840
displayed on a pike above the&nbsp;
Red Keep. Along with Ned's head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:37.040
several other members of the Stark household and&nbsp;
guard in King's Landing are also stuck on spikes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:42.720
nearby. Apparently, one time U.S. President&nbsp;
George W. Bush was part of the Stark retinue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:46.960
because his head was impaled on a&nbsp;
pike right next to Sansa's old septa.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.120
The showrunners explained that the use of&nbsp;
President Bush's head wasn't intentional but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.600
rather a budget issue of just using whatever&nbsp;
prop heads were available at the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.480
Nonetheless, some viewers took issue with the&nbsp;
scene, which prompted HBO to issue an apology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:06.601
and digitally edit the face on the head to make&nbsp;
it less recognizable in future DVD releases.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:12.880
George R. R. Martin is a known fan of the British&nbsp;
comedy troupe Monty Python — particularly their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:18.000
movie, Monty Python and the Holy Grail. References&nbsp;
to the film are sprinkled throughout the books,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.640
and the showrunners made sure to include some in&nbsp;
the television series as well. In the Season 4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:28.080
episode "Breaker of Chains," Daenerys arrives at&nbsp;
the city of Meereen with her army of Unsullied.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:33.440
The Meereenese send out a champion, who hurls&nbsp;
several insults at the Dragon Queen before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.240
Daario beheads the poor fool. According to&nbsp;
show linguist David Peterson, the beginning of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:44.434
his speech translates as a direct quote from the&nbsp;
French Taunter in Monty Python and the Holy Grail.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:50.334
"Your mother was a hamster, and&nbsp;
your father smelt of elderberries."

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:55.680
Long before the Tower of Joy scene in the Season&nbsp;
6 finale finally confirmed the long-held fan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:00.880
theory that Jon Snow is the son of Rhaegar&nbsp;
Targaryen and Lyanna Stark, Game of Thrones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:05.600
made a sneaky reference to the theory with a&nbsp;
set piece. In the Season 1 episode "Cripples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:10.640
Bastards, and Broken Things," Jon Snow&nbsp;
and Samwell Tarly are scrubbing a table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:15.440
and joking around about their past experiences&nbsp;
with women. Additionally, Jon reveals that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:19.680
doesn't know who his mother was. If you watch&nbsp;
closely during a scene from the same episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:24.400
you can see the letters "R" and "L" carved&nbsp;
into the post that Jon is standing next to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.534
which seems to tease us by&nbsp;
confirming the idea that R+L = J.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:34.720
On several occasions, famous bands or musicians&nbsp;
found their way into Game of Thrones in cameo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:39.760
roles. Coldplay drummer Will Champion played a&nbsp;
percussionist in the group of Frey musicians at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:44.320
the Red Wedding, whilst Sigur Ros made an&nbsp;
appearance as another set of minstrels at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:48.800
Joffrey and Margaery's Purple Wedding. Gary&nbsp;
Lightbody, the singer from Snow Patrol,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:55.200
made a cameo as a singer with Vargo Hoat's "Brave&nbsp;
Companions" in Season 3. Finally, the rockers of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:01.280
Mastodon got an enviable and totally metal scene&nbsp;
as wildlings at Hardhome who get brutally killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:06.320
before rising again as the wight thralls of the&nbsp;
White Walkers. Additionally, singer-songwriter Ed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:11.120
Sheeran made waves with a controversial cameo&nbsp;
in the first episode of the seventh season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:16.005
playing a friendly Lannister soldier named&nbsp;
Eddie whom Arya encounters on the Kingsroad.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:21.440
Musician Ed Sheeran's Season 7 cameo as the&nbsp;
Lannister soldier Eddie was poorly received.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:24.320
Many viewers thought Sheeran's&nbsp;
presence felt shoehorned in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:28.400
and ultimately crippled the show's sense&nbsp;
of reality, resulting in a multitude of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:32.720
scathing reactions and comments. In&nbsp;
response, Sheeran even told Buzzfeed,

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:35.440
"I definitely think they should've&nbsp;
killed me off in the episode. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:39.120
reckon that probably would've been the&nbsp;
redemption for people that didn't like it."

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:44.080
The good news for fans, critics, and Sheeran is&nbsp;
that their collective wish came at least partially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:49.520
true in the form of a subtle reference during the&nbsp;
Season 8 premiere. The Easter egg comes during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:53.760
scene in which Ser Bronn is in bed with three&nbsp;
courtesans. While they disrobe, the three women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:58.400
recount some of their favorite Lannister men who&nbsp;
either never came home from war or, in Eddie's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:04.320
case, came home horribly disfigured. They refer&nbsp;
to Eddie by name, even calling him "the ginger."

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.000
"Came back with his face burnt&nbsp;
right off. He's got no eyelids now."

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:11.840
Remember that time when Littlefinger gave&nbsp;
away the rest of the season's major deaths&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:16.000
in a single sentence? In the Season 4&nbsp;
episode "The Mountain and the Viper,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.560
Littlefinger gives some sage advice to&nbsp;
Robin Arryn at the Eyrie, telling him,

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:24.560
"People die at their dinner&nbsp;
tables. They die in their beds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.680
They die squatting over their chamber pots."

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:32.480
The first part is clearly a reference to&nbsp;
King Joffrey, who was already dead at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:36.800
point. But the latter two refer to events that&nbsp;
Littlefinger could have no foreknowledge of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:41.840
unless he's psychic. Later that season, Tyrion&nbsp;
would kill his former lover Shae in a bed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:47.434
and his father Tywin while he was squatting over&nbsp;
his chamber pot. Thanks for the spoilers, Baelish.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:52.880
When the young Ned Stark enters the Tower of Joy&nbsp;
after defeating Arthur Dayne, he leans Dayne's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:57.600
sword up against the foot of Lyanna's bed as&nbsp;
he approaches her. For book fans who were eager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:02.720
to see a glimpse of the fabled sword Dawn, the&nbsp;
camera lingers for a moment on the bloody sword.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:07.600
Beyond this visual Easter egg, for some Game of&nbsp;
Thrones theorists, the presence of the sword at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:13.600
Jon's birth confirms the theory that Jon Snow is&nbsp;
actually Azor Ahai — the Prince that was Promised,

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:17.360
"Born amidst salt and smoke,&nbsp;
beneath a bleeding star."

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:22.240
As readers will be quick to inform you, the&nbsp;
Dayne ancestral sword of Dawn was supposedly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:27.680
forged from the heart of a star, and the sword is&nbsp;
most definitely covered in blood. Is it a stretch?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:33.440
Possibly. But what else did fans have to speculate&nbsp;
about once Jon's parentage was finally made clear?

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:38.880
For horrified fans of House Stark who watched the&nbsp;
downfall of Robb and Catelyn at the Red Wedding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:44.480
revenge couldn't come soon enough. It took several&nbsp;
seasons, but the payback was truly worth the wait.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:49.280
During the Red Wedding, Robb Stark’s wife&nbsp;
is stabbed in the belly by Lothar Frey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:54.160
Robb is stabbed in the heart by Roose Bolton,&nbsp;
Catelyn has her throat slit by Walder Rivers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:58.960
and Robb’s dire wolf Grey Wind is killed&nbsp;
with crossbows. The deaths of the Red Wedding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:03.280
conspirators match those of their victims&nbsp;
— Tywin Lannister is shot with a crossbow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:06.480
Roose Bolton is stabbed in the gut,&nbsp;
and Walder Frey's throat is slit by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:11.200
Arya Stark. For the Stark family,&nbsp;
justice is both poetic and sweet.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:15.680
While staying at the Nightfort along&nbsp;
the Wall with Jojen, Meera, and Hodor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:21.600
Bran relates the ancient tale of the Rat Cook,&nbsp;
as told to him by Old Nan. Hundreds of years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:27.440
an Andal king was visiting the Nightfort with his&nbsp;
son. A cook at the Night's Watch castle was angry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:32.960
with the king for some supposed wrong and murdered&nbsp;
the prince and baked him into a pie with bacon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:38.000
which he then served to the king. The king was so&nbsp;
pleased with the pie that he asked for a second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:43.600
piece. According to the legend, the old gods were&nbsp;
so outraged by this that they cursed the cook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:47.920
turning him into a giant white rat&nbsp;
that can only survive by eating his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:52.320
own young. Supposedly the rat still roams&nbsp;
the Nightfort today. According to Bran,

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:55.120
"It was not for murder that&nbsp;
the gods cursed the Rat Cook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:57.600
nor for serving the king's son in a pie."

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:02.800
The tale is still told in Westeros as a warning&nbsp;
as to what happens to those who break guests'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:07.200
rights — like Walder Frey. His eventual&nbsp;
death echoes many aspects of the story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:11.360
Before killing him, Arya Stark kills two&nbsp;
of his sons and cooks them into a pie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:16.480
which she then serves to Walder Frey. It isn't&nbsp;
obvious in the scene, but if you look closely, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:21.600
can see that Walder already has the scraps of one&nbsp;
piece of pie on his plate, and the piece that Arya&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:28.034
brings him is his second. Revenge is truly a dish&nbsp;
best served cold — and full of dismembered toes.

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:34.334
"He killed a guest beneath his roof, and&nbsp;
that's something the gods can't forgive."

00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:39.760
During Season 7, a mountain that "looks like&nbsp;
an arrowhead" became a pivotal landmark when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:44.880
Jon and his band of merry men went beyond the&nbsp;
Wall in search of a wight to capture. Seen by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:49.680
Sandor Clegane during a vision, the mountain is&nbsp;
used by the group to guide them on their journey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.000
It wasn't the first time we'd&nbsp;
seen the mountain on the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:55.840
however — it also loomed in the&nbsp;
background of Bran's Season 6 vision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:00.640
when he watched the Children of the Forest create&nbsp;
the first White Walker. At that point in time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:06.160
the area surrounding the peak was lush and green,&nbsp;
not the frozen tundra it is now. In reality, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:11.601
mountain is a popular landmark in Iceland, where&nbsp;
a large portion of Game of Thrones is filmed.

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:13.840
Although the Boltons are now dead and gone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:18.240
the damage they did to the Stark family&nbsp;
goes much deeper than psychological trauma.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:22.960
We got a glimpse of that trauma in the&nbsp;
second episode of Season 7, when Jon leaves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:27.520
the Winterfell crypt after a tense conversation&nbsp;
with Littlefinger. If you were paying attention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:31.920
you may have noticed the headless statues&nbsp;
standing guard outside the crypt's entrance.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:35.920
What's the significance of these&nbsp;
broken guardians? As seen in Season 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:41.040
two sturdy and detailed dire wolf statues&nbsp;
stood on either side of the crypt entrance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:45.360
Theon Greyjoy may have held Winterfell&nbsp;
with his Ironborn for a brief time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:50.240
but it wasn't until the Boltons took up residence&nbsp;
that these statues were desecrated. The Boltons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:54.560
were trying to replace the Starks as the new&nbsp;
power in the North, and by beheading the statues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:59.760
they were reminding the people of Winterfell who&nbsp;
was in charge. In addition, these beheaded statues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:04.480
underscore for the viewer what has been lost;&nbsp;
the remaining Stark siblings have been reunited,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:10.000
but so many others have died. In fact, two&nbsp;
of those deaths — Robb's and Rickon's — came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:14.834
directly at the hands of the Boltons,&nbsp;
making this an even more poignant reminder.

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:19.760
During the course of Season 7, a certain&nbsp;
Valyrian steel knife known as the "Catspaw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:24.800
dagger" came into play on multiple occasions.&nbsp;
First, Littlefinger gave the knife to Bran, who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:30.080
then passed it on to Arya. As Sansa explained in&nbsp;
the season finale, Littlefinger told Catelyn that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:35.440
the dagger had belonged to Tyrion Lannister–which&nbsp;
caused Catelyn to kidnap Tyrion, Tywin Lannister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:40.320
to go to war, and Jaime Lannister to attack&nbsp;
Ned Stark in the streets of King's Landing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:45.440
In reality, it was Littlefinger's dagger, and&nbsp;
his lies had been designed to create chaos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:49.280
But the story of this particular&nbsp;
dagger actually goes back much further.

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:54.160
When Samwell Tarly is researching Dragonglass&nbsp;
in Oldtown, he flips through the pages of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:58.720
book about the topic. Actor John Bradley told&nbsp;
the Huffington Post that during the scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:03.600
he was directed to "linger on this page" so&nbsp;
audiences could get a good look, signifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:08.400
that the reference is an important one. On one&nbsp;
page is an illustration of the Catspaw dagger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:13.760
with text indicating that it's made of Valyrian&nbsp;
steel with Dragonglass ornamentation — meaning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:18.400
it's made of the two materials that can kill&nbsp;
White Walkers. Another clue hidden on that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:23.600
page suggests that the Catspaw dagger may once&nbsp;
have belonged to Aegon the Conqueror himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:27.840
When Bran received the dagger from Littlefinger,&nbsp;
he asked him an important question.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:29.840
"Do you know who this belonged to?"

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:35.280
This question has much more meaning now that&nbsp;
we know it may have been Aegon the First's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:39.680
and that Jon's true name is also Aegon&nbsp;
Targaryen. And the dagger's long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:43.440
bloody history may yet continue, as&nbsp;
the weapon seems to make an appearance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:48.468
in a trailer for House of the Dragon,&nbsp;
wielded by a member of House Hightower.

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:53.200
In the Season 7 episode "Beyond the Wall,"&nbsp;
Daenerys is forced to rescue Jon and his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:57.120
crew when their mission to trap a wight ends&nbsp;
with them in the middle of a frozen lake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:01.120
surrounded by the army of the dead.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, Jon gets pulled into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:06.320
the icy waters before Dany can save him.&nbsp;
Luckily, he emerges mostly unscathed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:11.360
and his Uncle Benjen unexpectedly shows up —&nbsp;
giving Jon his horse and sacrificing himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:16.080
to the horde of wights to give his nephew a&nbsp;
head start. When Jon arrives back at Eastwatch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:20.800
he's hypothermic and is quickly brought aboard&nbsp;
their ship, where Daenerys watches in horror as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:25.600
she sees Jon's old scars from when he was&nbsp;
murdered by the Night's Watch mutineers.

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:29.520
This Easter egg is all about those&nbsp;
scars — or rather, their placement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:34.720
Daenerys' lost love Khal Drogo had a scar&nbsp;
through his left eyebrow, as does Jon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:38.560
Additionally, Drogo died from an&nbsp;
infected wound very near his heart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:43.680
and Jon has a puckered scar in the same place.&nbsp;
The similarities between these scars may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:49.120
contributed to Daenerys' shock when she saw Jon's&nbsp;
wounds.It likely brought up horrible memories of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:54.320
how her first husband died. Would she lose Jon the&nbsp;
same way? It's clear from the following scene that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:59.440
she'd been sitting at Jon's bedside for hours,&nbsp;
just as she did for Drogo when he was near death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:04.534
hoping against hope that he'd return&nbsp;
to her. This time, she got her wish.

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:10.640
Fans rejoiced when Ser Davos Seaworth tracked&nbsp;
down Gendry in the Season 7 episode "Eastwatch,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:14.800
and found out that he hadn't actually been&nbsp;
rowing for the last four seasons. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:18.720
Gendry was working for himself in the&nbsp;
Street of Steel at King's Landing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:23.840
When Davos asks Gendry to come back to&nbsp;
Dragonstone, Gendry jumps at the chance — only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:28.400
pausing to grab an exquisitely crafted war&nbsp;
hammer. Gendry is pretty darn good with it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:32.640
too, as seen when he smashes in&nbsp;
the heads of two nosy Gold Cloaks.

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:37.200
Gendry's war hammer was embellished with&nbsp;
a golden stag, which is the sigil of House&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.200 --> 00:13:41.920
Baratheon. When Robert was in his prime, he&nbsp;
was known for his prowess with a war hammer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.920 --> 00:13:46.960
which he used to kill Rhaegar Targaryen. It's&nbsp;
likely that Gendry knows this, which may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:51.760
why he made himself the weapon — a reference to&nbsp;
his father's favored hammer. What's really fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:57.360
about all of this is the initial meeting between&nbsp;
Gendry and Jon, when Gendry tells Jon something.

00:13:57.668 --> 00:14:00.388
"Our fathers trusted each&nbsp;
other; why shouldn't we?"

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:05.520
Gendry was referring to Robert and Ned, but we&nbsp;
know Jon's real father was Rhaegar Targaryen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:08.601
and Gendry's father actually killed Jon's.

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:12.880
A fun fact about Game of Thrones that may&nbsp;
have eluded even the hardcore fans is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:18.400
the franchise's world has no official name. In&nbsp;
fact, characters in both the books and TV series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.400 --> 00:14:24.240
simply refer to the planet as "the world" or&nbsp;
"the known world," with no alternative proposed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:27.680
That's because George R.R. Martin&nbsp;
always intended the books to focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.680 --> 00:14:32.240
almost exclusively on one of the world's&nbsp;
continents. That continent is Westeros,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:37.200
and aside from brief forays into the far&nbsp;
western shore of Essos, Westeros is home to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.200 --> 00:14:41.680
all of the events of the series. And, as one&nbsp;
observant fan noticed, that home bears an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:47.200
uncanny resemblance to the islands of Ireland&nbsp;
and Great Britain stacked on top of another.

00:14:47.200 --> 00:14:50.240
Martin himself has always been&nbsp;
outspoken about his desire to base&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:54.240
his setting on the British Isles, even&nbsp;
describing the known world like this:

00:14:54.240 --> 00:14:57.920
"A super-Europe-super-Asia&nbsp;
and a giant British Isles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:01.440
and, you know, like they would have&nbsp;
actually known in the Middle Ages."

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:05.440
He was, of course, referring to Essos&nbsp;
and Westeros respectively. But to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:11.234
Westeros so neatly aligned with the two largest&nbsp;
British Isles is still a striking reference.

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:15.440
One way Game of Thrones sets itself apart&nbsp;
from other fantasy franchises is keeping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:20.720
its mythological creatures comparatively limited&nbsp;
in number. There are only a handful of different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:25.520
fantasy beasts that appear throughout the series,&nbsp;
and the very first non-human characters to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:30.560
appear — the Stark children's iconic dire&nbsp;
wolves — aren't actually mythical at all.

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:36.080
Dire wolves were a real species that interacted&nbsp;
with humans and went extinct only recently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:40.720
That fact helps lend a primal, visceral depth to&nbsp;
the way the Game of Thrones wolves connect with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.720 --> 00:15:46.400
the Starks. It's a joyous moment when Arya and&nbsp;
her wolf Nymeria finally reconnect in Season 7,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:50.640
although the pair almost immediately part&nbsp;
ways for reasons that might confuse viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:55.200
whose memories of Season 1 are a little&nbsp;
fuzzy. Arya says something odd to Nymeria.

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:58.560
"That's not you."

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:02.160
From this, fans could've gotten the idea&nbsp;
that this interaction is a simple case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.160 --> 00:16:06.800
of mistaken identity. In fact, it's a&nbsp;
subtle Easter egg years in the making.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.800 --> 00:16:10.080
Arya isn't claiming to have&nbsp;
confused the wolf for her own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.080 --> 00:16:14.880
but rather calling back to a line of dialogue from&nbsp;
Season 1, when she said this to her father Ned.

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:16.560
"That's not me."

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:23.280
This was in response to him trying to sell her&nbsp;
on the traditionally feminine life of a princess.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:28.080
Just as Arya knew that her path wasn't going&nbsp;
to be decided by others, she knew that Nymeria&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:33.734
was going to choose her own path as well — even&nbsp;
if it meant creating a new family without her.

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:38.080
It's no secret that George R. R.&nbsp;
Martin loves J. R. R. Tolkien's work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:40.560
with the Game of Thrones author even claiming that

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:43.920
"I yield to no one in my admiration&nbsp;
for 'Lord of the Rings.'"

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:49.280
Even when Martin finds himself in competition with&nbsp;
Tolkien's franchise, such as in the simultaneously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:54.000
airing seasons of House of the Dragon and&nbsp;
Rings of Power, he can't help but hope that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:59.760
the Tolkien-inspired show succeeds, even while&nbsp;
hoping House of the Dragon succeeds slightly more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:03.520
That's why many fans have been quick to&nbsp;
notice certain parallels between Martin's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:09.200
characters and Tolkien's, especially their&nbsp;
two main protagonists: Jon Snow and Frodo.

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:13.360
Both are orphans raised by surrogate&nbsp;
fathers. Both have best friends named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.360 --> 00:17:18.160
Sam and another bestie whose name is Pip,&nbsp;
possibly spelled with a "y". Both enter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:22.640
the realm of death after being stabbed&nbsp;
only to return via magical intervention.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:27.280
Both still feel the mortal wounds they endured&nbsp;
years later. Both save the world from what is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.280 --> 00:17:32.000
ultimately its greatest evil. Both are unable&nbsp;
to find peace or happiness in their newly-saved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:37.440
world. And both eventually leave their normal&nbsp;
world to start new lives in a realm of even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:43.201
fantasy. In the latter case, that's the Undying&nbsp;
Lands for Frodo and Beyond the Wall for Snow.

00:17:43.360 --> 00:17:47.760
Anyone who finds themselves in charge of one&nbsp;
of the biggest and most beloved fantasy series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:52.720
of all time must feel constant temptation to&nbsp;
cast themselves in the show. After spending the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:57.280
majority of their waking moments for years&nbsp;
imagining and creating a fictional world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.280 --> 00:18:02.480
those with the ability to put themselves inside&nbsp;
must seriously consider it. For Game of Thrones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.480 --> 00:18:07.760
showrunners David Benioff and D.B. Weiss,&nbsp;
the temptation was simply too much to resist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.760 --> 00:18:12.960
Their first cameo comes when Arya visits the&nbsp;
Hall of Faces. Molds of both showrunners' faces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:17.280
appear among the many that fill that&nbsp;
ghastly room. As Weiss once revealed,

00:18:17.280 --> 00:18:21.040
"Both David and myself appeared many,&nbsp;
many times in the Hall of Faces...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:23.911
there is at least 20 or 30 of me in there."

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:30.080
We've covered a huge number of cameos already, but&nbsp;
one important cameo remains thus far unmentioned:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.080 --> 00:18:35.040
Rob McElhenney, the creator, writer, and&nbsp;
co-star of It's Always Sunny in Philadelphia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:38.400
Though he's best known for playing&nbsp;
Mac on the brilliant FX comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:42.320
McElhenney also landed a role on Game&nbsp;
of Thrones in the Season 8 premiere.

00:18:42.320 --> 00:18:47.120
During Theon's mission to rescue Yara, her&nbsp;
loyalists launch a quick, silent assault on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.120 --> 00:18:52.480
the Ironborn ship in which she's held captive. The&nbsp;
opening moments of the attack see five Ironborn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.480 --> 00:18:57.600
sailors struck by arrows before they can even&nbsp;
react. The first of these sailors is none other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.600 --> 00:19:03.120
than McElhenney, who receives enough of a closeup&nbsp;
to confirm his role. However, like so many in Game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:08.400
of Thrones, McElhenney's character — known only&nbsp;
as "Ironborn 1" — was always fated to die. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:14.101
arrow that strikes him sails through his head and&nbsp;
out his eye, killing him brutally and instantly.

00:19:14.240 --> 00:19:19.680
If you haven't seen Disney's 1981 live-action&nbsp;
fantasy film Dragonslayer, do yourself a favor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:24.000
and watch it immediately. It's well reviewed,&nbsp;
a cult favorite, and an inspiration to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.000 --> 00:19:29.120
many industry professionals. In particular,&nbsp;
the film's dragon — Vermithrax Pejorative — has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:34.160
left an indelible mark on some major names.&nbsp;
Guillermo del Toro, for one, called the dragon

00:19:34.160 --> 00:19:37.440
"[...] one of the most perfect&nbsp;
creature designs ever made."

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:43.040
Another fantasy big-shot who forever has eyes&nbsp;
for Vermithrax is the author of Thrones himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:45.120
George R. R. Martin, who called the creature

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:49.280
"[...] the best dragon ever put on film,&nbsp;
and has the coolest dragon name as well."

00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:53.840
D.B. Weiss also shares Martin's love for&nbsp;
the wonderfully realistic dragon, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:59.200
why he snuck in an Easter egg to reference it in&nbsp;
Season 1. While Viserys is bathing with a woman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:03.920
he tells her about his childhood walking among&nbsp;
the skulls of dragons past in the Red Keep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.920 --> 00:20:08.000
Among the names of long-dead dragons&nbsp;
comes — you guessed it — Vermithrax.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:14.000
On the episode's Blu-ray commentary, writer Bryan&nbsp;
Cogman confirmed that the homage was Weiss's idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:18.000
an intentional nod to "one of&nbsp;
the great '80s fantasy films."

00:20:18.240 --> 00:20:23.040
No part of Game of Thrones exists without adding&nbsp;
to the show's constant storytelling and world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:27.840
building. So even the characters' outfits&nbsp;
were designed to convey specific reactions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:32.640
emotions, and character traits. Speaking with&nbsp;
Elle, costume designer Michele Clapton revealed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:32.640 --> 00:20:38.160
a few of her more noticeable contributions to&nbsp;
the final seasons' characters and tone. She said,

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:42.560
"Winter has come [...] People say 'Where's the&nbsp;
color gone?' If the end of the world was coming as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:42.560 --> 00:20:47.920
you know it, you wouldn't actually be colorful and&nbsp;
bright. You'd start being incredibly practical."

00:20:47.920 --> 00:20:52.800
That shows in many of the female characters'&nbsp;
clothes, which gradually shifted from bright&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:57.280
colors and flowing fabrics to blacks and&nbsp;
grays wrapped tightly around the body.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.280 --> 00:21:00.560
Clapton also revealed many&nbsp;
Easter eggs in Sansa's outfits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.560 --> 00:21:04.160
and the subtle references they make to&nbsp;
the character's greatest influences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:08.320
including her mother and father. They&nbsp;
even hint at the influences Sansa took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.320 --> 00:21:13.840
from Cersei and Littlefinger. The outfits also&nbsp;
reflect Sansa's traumas or, as Clapton put it,

00:21:13.840 --> 00:21:18.560
"I wanted it to look as if she was actually&nbsp;
sewn or laced into her dress — like there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.560 --> 00:21:23.120
no way in. She's trying to protect herself&nbsp;
from all the awfulness that's happened."

00:21:23.120 --> 00:21:26.080
Of course, this is all even more&nbsp;
impressive when you consider the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:26.080 --> 00:21:29.818
fact that some of the wardrobe is just IKEA rugs.

00:21:29.840 --> 00:21:33.280
There are many facets of Game of&nbsp;
Thrones, from its myriad characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.280 --> 00:21:37.840
intricately interwoven storylines, and&nbsp;
expansive scope of in-universe history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:42.800
But, the series' single greatest source of&nbsp;
Easter eggs comes from one surprising place:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.800 --> 00:21:48.240
its opening animation sequence. The sequence,&nbsp;
widely hailed as one of the most epic of all time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:53.120
and an Emmy winner itself, travels across&nbsp;
the map of the known world and changes from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.120 --> 00:21:57.680
episode to episode, adding more Easter&nbsp;
eggs for eagle-eyed fans to unearth.

00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:01.520
The sequence's many variations share one&nbsp;
of the most elaborate pages on the Game of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:06.560
Thrones wiki page, and with good reason — they&nbsp;
not only chronicle the history of the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:09.760
but they also tease its future. As Kirk Shintani,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.760 --> 00:22:13.200
part of the team at Elastic that&nbsp;
created the sequence, told The Wrap,

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:17.200
"As the episodes progress, you know, as the&nbsp;
set piece changes, the things that you notice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:17.200 --> 00:22:21.840
happen later, you might find little hints of&nbsp;
those same things in the previous episodes."

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:26.240
As a specific example, he cited the&nbsp;
revamped Season 8 sequence, saying:

00:22:26.240 --> 00:22:29.600
"Everything that is going to be there&nbsp;
for all six episodes. Those elements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.600 --> 00:22:33.490
will have been in the Season 8 opening&nbsp;
credits from the premiere episode."

